# Resolution
Fixed as of 2026-08-04 by the item 13 storage-boundary containment change.

The earlier acceptance has been superseded. Raw storage RPC messages no longer
enter HTTP 500 diagnostics, the flight recorder, panic-on-500, or abort-on-500
output. Storage classifies every local, nested shard, and RPC failure
exhaustively through two independent bounded values:

- `StoreOperationFailureClass` carries only the semantic information required
  for request outcome and retry policy.
- A private storage-owned diagnostic category distinguishes broad operator
  domains such as I/O, database, integrity, topology, schema, metadata
  consistency, resource exhaustion, and RPC transport/protocol failures.

The operator label contains no bucket or object name, node/PG/epoch identity,
path, database text, RPC operation, remote message, or nested cause. Public
`StoreFailure` formatting is bounded, its `Display` is generic, and
`Error::source()` is deliberately empty. The bucket-drain failure flight record
emits the bounded label rather than formatting its owner-local wrapper.
Owner-local and cross-crate flight-recorder regressions verify that the
categories remain useful while sensitive implementation text is absent.

The subsequent item 14 structural-containment work completed on 2026-08-08.
`StoreError`, `MetadataError`, `ObjectPgActionError`, `ShardIoError`, and the
module defining them are now crate-private. Public startup wrappers consume
concrete causes into `StoreFailure`, and all other public operations return
logical or bounded opaque failures. The boundary checker pins those private
declarations and removed exports and rejects renewed concrete-error use outside
storage.

# Summary
Introduced: the commit propagates raw storage RPC diagnostic messages into HTTP 500 diagnostics and emits them to server-side observability sinks without redacting or hashing sensitive request-derived fields.
The new server_storage_rpc_detail path formats StoreError::StorageRpc messages verbatim and emits them for HTTP 500 responses. Storage RPC error messages are not guaranteed to be redacted; existing storage-node validation and error conversion can include bucket names, object keys, and internal PG/node/epoch information in those messages. The value is not sent in normal S3 response bodies, but it is recorded in the in-memory flight recorder and optional trace output, and can later be dumped to stderr on panic/abort-on-500. This weakens the existing redaction model, where storage RPC error observability previously logged only message length/hash rather than raw content.

